<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Edfu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">237–<a href="/years/57-bce">57 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Apollonopolis Magna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> is an Egyptian temple on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> at Edfu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> between 237 and 57 BCE and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>. Begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes">Ptolemy III Euergetes</a> and completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII Auletes</a> on the site of an earlier temple of Horus, it is one of the best preserved shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the largest temple dedicated to Horus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> of Dendera. Its inscribed walls record its building, its creation myth and the conflict between Horus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>, and each year Hathor's journey from Dendera to Edfu was marked by a great festival. Buried up to 12 metres deep in sand and silt, it was cleared from 1860 by Auguste Mariette.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-edfu.jpg" alt="Temple of Edfu" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrick.reb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Edfou_Egypte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> was one of several temples built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>, among them those at Dendera, Esna, Kom Ombo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, and its size reflects the prosperity of the time. The present temple was begun on 23 August 237 BCE and at first consisted of a pillared hall, two transverse halls and a barque sanctuary surrounded by chapels.</p><p>Work started under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes">Ptolemy III Euergetes</a> and was completed in 57 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII Auletes</a>. It stands on the site of an earlier, smaller temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, which was oriented east-west rather than north-south, and inscriptions on a ruined pylon to the east point to building under Ramesses I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The inscribed walls give details of the temple's construction and preserve the mythical reading of it, and of all temples, as the Island of Creation. According to the creation myth on the inner face of the perimeter wall, the world was at first covered by water. Reeds grew where the land neared the surface, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> landed there as a falcon, which began the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a>. The serpent Apophis tried to impede creation but was defeated, and a falcon formed the sky dome. The gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a> and Seshat then planned the first temple, built at first of reed, and sixty gods formed a living wall to protect it.</p><p>The temple was the largest dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> of Dendera, and it was the centre of several festivals sacred to Horus. Each year Hathor travelled south from Dendera to visit Horus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a>, an event marking their sacred marriage and the occasion of a great festival and pilgrimage. The walls also carry scenes of the Sacred Drama, the age-old conflict between Horus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Over the centuries the temple was buried to a depth of 12 metres beneath drifting sand and silt left by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, and local people built houses over its grounds. By 1798, when a French expedition identified it, only the tops of the pylons showed. In 1860 the French Egyptologist Auguste Mariette began to free it from the sands. It is nearly intact and a good example of an ancient Egyptian temple, and a naos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a> from an earlier building survives in its inner sanctuary. Its columns were copied in the frontage of the Temple Works in Holbeck, Leeds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temple of Edfu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
